# MyAutoClick
An AutoHotKey script to automate a left mouse click when the mouse cursor stops moving for a specified time period.

MyAutoClick.ini must present in the same folder for MyAutoClick to work.  The initial MyAutoClick.ini is created by running MyAutoClick Parameters.exe (which was compiled using the MyAutoClick Parameters.ahk AutoHotKey script).

Initial setup:
- Run MyAutoClick Parameters.exe
  - Enter the wait time (in milliseconds) the mouse cursor must pause before an autoclick occurs.  For example, 1000 = 1 second.
  - Enter the polling time (in milliseconds) MyAutoClick checks to see if the mouse cursor is moving or stationary.  10 should be a good initial polling time.
  - Press the hotkey combination used to toggle MyAutoClick on and off. (Examples: F10, Shift+Ctrl+Alt+S, etc.)

After running MyAutoClick Parameters.exe, MyAutoClick.ini should contain the following settings:
- WaitTime - How long the mouse cursor must remain stationary before a click is registered (in milliseconds).
- PollingTime - How often MyAutoClick checks for mouse cursor movement (in milliseconds).
- Hkey - Key combination used to toggle MyAutoClick on and off.

When first running MyAutoClick, a red mouse cursor icon will display in the System Tray (which may display in the hidden icon area) indicating that MyAutoClick is running, but toggled off.  

By pressing the hotkey (created when running MyAutoClick Parameters.exe), the red mouse cursor icon will be replaced with a green mouse cursor icon indicating that MyAutoClick is toggled on.

To exit MyAutoClick, right click the red or green mouse cursor icon in the System Tray and select Exit.

